  "query_preview": "..\n\nLingering elevated shower and thunderstorms are moving across \ncentral/northern lower Michigan and Lake Huron early this morning. \nThis activity has been forced by moist isentropic ascent with the \nincreasing nocturnal jet atop a west to east frontal zone. Elevated \ninstability brought embedded thunder as this activity rolled \nthrough. Bulk of activity has pushed east of the forecast area with \na few lingering showers and thunderstorms expected to be out of the \narea by 8 am.  \n\nStill good agreement that a period of drying will occur in the wake \nof this mornings precipitation as surface low pressure tracks across \nand east of northern Lower Michigan. With skies clearing across much \nof the area for bulk of the peak heating period, high temperatures \nwill again make a run into the 80s as S-SW winds maintain warm \nadvection for one more day. The sagging cold front will hold off \nlong enough across the south for high temperatures to make a run at \nthe record high temperature for October 13th at DTW (see climate \nsection). Mixing ahead of the cold front and tightened pressure \ngradient will bring wind gusts this afternoon up to 25-30 mph. Cold \nfront settles along the southern MI border this evening allowing \novernight lows to fall into the 50s. \n\nThis cold front is forecast to stall out around the southern MI \nborder tonight into Wednesday while the gulf ridge holds in place. \nThis will maintain a west-southwest flow regime allowing for \nadditional gulf moisture to stream into the Ohio Valley. Shortwave \nenergy is forecast to eject out of the Rockies east of a closed mid \nlevel low along this frontal zone developing additional focused \nsouth of I-69 starting by or shortly after midnight and continuing \nthrough morning with greater focus along MI/OH border with time. \nLimited instability north of the frontal boundary brings a low \nchance for a rumble of thunder mainly during the overnight period. \nPost frontal airmass will offer afternoon temperatures in the 60s \nfor most of the forecast area. It looks like dry conditions will \nprevail for Wednesday afternoon as surface high pressure moves \nacross the upper midwest bringing northerly flow that will drive the \nnearby boundary further south.  \n\nMore typical fall temperatures expected to hold into Thursday. A \nmerging split flow pattern with a strong Canadian trough and a wave \nlifting out of the central plains will yield an influx of more gulf \nmoisture as the short term pattern becomes increasingly more dynamic \nover the Great Lakes. A surge of likely widespread rainfall will \npush into southeast Michigan Thursday afternoon into Thursday night \nwith a cold front sweeping through Friday morning. In addition to \nthe strong cold advection behind the front and the potential for \nlingering lake enhanced rain showers, expect a windy Friday as gusts \nmay reach 30-40 mph. Temperatures towards -8C at 850 mb arrive \nFriday night offering temperatures down into the low 30s to start \nSaturday morning. Drier conditions develop Saturday with \ntemperatures still about 10 degrees below normal for Saturday \nafternoon. Inbound high amplitude ridge does allow for a rather \nquick recovery back towards more normal temperatures for Sunday.",
